task:
Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the shipments with item `tank` by its `units`: `bottom` if it is less than 4, `middle` if it is less than 12, and `top` otherwise. Print the number of shipments in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`;
2. for each client calculates the total cost (`units` × `unit_cost`), considering only shipments from the month `2026-02`, and then prints the 4 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `client: X USD`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. classifies every one of the shipments from the month `2026-04` by its `weight`: `good` if it is at least 55 and `poor` otherwise. Print the number of shipments in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class - count`;
2. determines the highest weight in kilograms (`weight`) over all shipments and prints just the value (first print the text `Report:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the total weight in kilograms (`weight`) for each month, considering only shipments where `unit_cost` is less than 30 and labels each result: `small` if it is less than 1540, `medium` if it is less than 2370, and `large` otherwise. Print the labels chronologically by month, in the format `month - label`;
2. works out the lowest unit cost (`unit_cost`) among shipments with result `delivered` and with region `east` or `south` and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. for each status computes the average number of items (`qty`) per order, and then prints the single entry with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `status: average`;
2. classifies every one of the orders by its `qty`: `light` if it is less than 2, `medium` if it is less than 3, and `heavy` otherwise. Print the number of orders in each class, alphabetically by class, in the format `class: count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the total weight in kilograms (`weight`) for each region and labels each result: `small` if it is less than 7090, `medium` if it is less than 10750, and `large` otherwise. Print the labels alphabetically by region, in the format `region: label`;
2. classifies every one of the shipments with result `returned` by its `units`: `above` if it is at least 6 and `below` otherwise. Print the number of shipments in each class, alphabetically by class, in the format `class: count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each month, the percentage of bookings whose room type name contains the fragment `it` among all bookings of that month, considering only bookings where `nights` is at least 2, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the results chronologically, one per line in the format `month: X%`;
2. computes the highest guest rating (`rating`) among bookings with city `paris` or `rome` and with room type `double` and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the average number of nights (`nights`) per booking for each month, and then prints chronologically only the entries whose value is at least 3.1, in the format `month: average` (first print the text `Overview:`);
2. computes the highest nightly rate (`rate`) over all bookings and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each month, its share of the total revenue (`qty` × `price`) over all orders, as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the 4 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `month: X%`;
2. determines the largest single order value (`qty` × `price`) for each customer, and then displays the results alphabetically, one per line in the format `customer - X USD` (first print the text `Report:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. works out the number of distinct categories for each month, and then outputs the 4 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `month: distinct` (first print the text `Summary:`);
2. computes the number of orders for each month and labels each result: `high` if it is at least 67 and `low` otherwise. Print the labels chronologically by month, in the format `month - label`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each result, the percentage of shipments with result `lost` among all shipments of that result, considering only shipments where `weight` is less than 20, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the 2 entries with the largest value (descending), one per line in the format `result: X%`;
2. classifies every one of the shipments by its value `units` × `unit_cost`: `small` if it is less than 50, `medium` if it is less than 222, and `large` otherwise. Print the number of shipments in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the average number of units (`units`) per shipment for each region and labels each result: `large` if it is at least 7.5 and `small` otherwise. Print the labels alphabetically by region, in the format `region: label`;
2. computes, for each month, the percentage of shipments with result `lost` among all shipments of that month, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints the results chronologically, one per line in the format `month - X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. prints, alphabetically and without duplicates, the values of the column `guest` for bookings with guest `dmitri` or `jonas` and whose room type name contains the fragment `ami`;
2. computes, for each guest, the percentage of bookings with outcome `cancelled` among all bookings of that guest, rounded to 1 decimal place, and then displays alphabetically only the entries whose value is at least 20, in the format `guest: X%`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the average booking value (`nights` × `rate`) for each guest, considering only bookings from the month `2026-02` and labels each result: `good` if it is greater than 440 and `poor` otherwise. Print the labels alphabetically by guest, in the format `guest: label`;
2. classifies every one of the bookings by its value `nights` × `rate`: `small` if it is less than 219, `medium` if it is less than 580, and `large` otherwise. Print the number of bookings in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each client, its share of the total cost (`units` × `unit_cost`) over all shipments, as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place, and then prints alphabetically only the entries whose value is at least 9.8, in the format `client: X%`;
2. determines the highest unit cost (`unit_cost`) among shipments whose item name contains the fragment `bun` and where `unit_cost` is less than 30 and prints just the value (first print the text `Data:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes the lowest nightly rate (`rate`) multiplied by 0.8 and rounded to 2 decimal places for every outcome, considering only bookings from the month `2026-05`, then outputs the 2 entries with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `outcome - value`;
2. determines the total booking value (`nights` × `rate`) over all bookings and prints just the value (first print the text `Data:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. prints, alphabetically and without duplicates, the values of the column `item` for shipments where `units` is at least 5 and with item `pallet` or `tank`;
2. computes the average number of units (`units`) per shipment for every item, then outputs the single entry with the smallest value (ascending), one per line in the format `item: average` (first print the text `Result:`).

Write a Ken program that:
1. computes, for each result, its share of the total number of units (`units`) over all shipments, as a percentage rounded to 1 decimal place, and then outputs alphabetically only the entries whose value is at least 12, in the format `result: X%`;
2. prints, alphabetically and without duplicates, the values of the column `item` for shipments from the month `2026-05`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. calculates the number of distinct categories for every product, considering only orders with status `done`, then displays all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `product - distinct`;
2. classifies every one of the orders where `qty` is greater than 2 by its `price`: `small` if it is less than 50, `medium` if it is less than 400, and `large` otherwise, using your own word `band` for the rule. Print the number of orders in each class, most numerous first, in the format `class: count`.

Write a Ken program that:
1. works out the total revenue (`qty` × `price`) for every product, then prints all results from the largest value to the smallest, in the format `product: X USD`;
2. calculates the average number of items (`qty`) per order over all orders and prints just the value.

Write a Ken program that:
1. prints, alphabetically and without duplicates, the values of the column `product` for orders from the month `2026-04` and with category `clothing` or `electronics`;
2. calculates the number of distinct categories among orders with category `clothing` or `groceries` and with status `returned` and prints just the value (first print the text `Summary:`).
